above, this forms the complete algorithmic frame-
work of NSA.
2.4 Kernel Design
To achieve FlashAttention-level speedup during
the training and prefilling, we implement hardware-
aligned sparse attention kernels upon Triton. Given
MHA is memory-intensive and inefficient for de-
coding, we focus on architectures with shared KV
caches like GQA and MQA following the current
state-of-the-art LLMs. While compression and
sliding window attention computations are read-
ily compatible with existing FlashAttention-2 ker-
nels, we introduce the specialized kernel design
for sparse selection attention. Figure 3 illustrates
the forward pass implementation. If we were to
follow FlashAttention’s strategy of loading tempo-
rally continuous query blocks into SRAM, it would
result in inefficient memory access since queries
within a block may require disjoint KV blocks. To
address this, our key optimization lies in a different
query grouping strategy: for each position on the
query sequence, we load all query heads within a
GQA group (they share the same sparse KV blocks)
into SRAM. The proposed kernel architecture is
characterized by the following key features:
1.Group-Centric Data Loading . For each in-
