5 Conclusion
We present NSA, a hardware-aware sparse atten-
tion architecture for efficient long-context model-
ing. By integrating hierarchical token compres-
sion with block selection within a trainable archi-
tecture, we achieve faster training and inference
while matching or surpassing Full Attention perfor-
mance. Our approach demonstrates that hardware-
aligned sparse attention can rival Full Attention im-
plementations in efficiency and performance. NSA
advances the state-of-the-art through: superior
base model performance, exceptional long-context
performance, and improved reasoning ability, all
while achieving significant reductions in compu-
tational latency. We present NSA, a hardware-23085

aligned sparse attention architecture for efficient
long-context modeling. By integrating hierarchi-
cal token compression with blockwise token selec-
tion within a trainable architecture, our architecture
achieves accelerated training and inference while
maintaining Full Attention performance. NSA ad-
vances the state-of-the-art by demonstrating gen-
eral benchmark performance matches full-attention
baselines, exceeding modeling capability in long-
context evaluations, and enhanced reasoning abil-
ity, all accompanied by measurable reductions in
computational latency.
Limitations
In this work, we focus on sparsity within atten-
tion maps of a single layer. Exploring cross-layer
sparsity remains an intriguing avenue for future
research. Also, our Triton-based implementation,
while introducing some abstraction overhead com-
pared to native CUDA kernels, offers opportunities
for further hardware optimization.
